# Build trees and toolchain. All regenerated; none of it belongs in history.
.tooling/
.cache/
build/
dist/
vcpkg_installed/

# Python: virtualenv, byte-code, and setuptools output. services/python/build
# and the .egg-info held a stale duplicate of the whole package, which is how a
# search comes back with two answers.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
services/python/build/

# Rust
native/target/

# Diagnostic output, regenerated on every run
full-log.txt
startup-*.txt
crash-report.txt
backups/

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
graph-check/

# Where files delivered from a Claude session land. Not just clutter: it holds
# second copies of latex.py, operations.py, science-tests.yml and test_fuzz.py,
# and a stale QtPlotBackend.cpp 300 KB smaller than the real one. A duplicate
# of a source file in the tree is the same fault as the .egg-info above - a
# search comes back with two answers and one of them is wrong.
Claude outputs/

# The 434-figure gallery, regenerated from the binary by MAKE-GALLERY.bat and
# by the sweep. Reproducible, binary, and every file changes at once.
gallery/

# Scaffolding for the first commit - the read-only reporter, the commit script
# and the message it reads. Ignored so they cannot commit themselves, and safe
# to delete once the commit is made.
GIT-STATUS.bat
COMMIT.bat
commit-message.txt

# Generated build reports. These are OUTPUT - a machine's paths, timings and
# staged sizes - rewritten on every run, and they were tracked, so each one put
# one developer's environment into everybody's diff. build-reports-README.md at
# the root explains what they are; the reports themselves are regenerated by
# tools/build_report.py and tools/passive_audit.py.
#
# The folder's own README stays tracked - it is the explanation of what these
# files are, and a reader who finds an empty ignored folder learns nothing. The
# `/*` spelling is deliberate: git does not descend into an ignored DIRECTORY,
# so `build-reports/` followed by a negation would not have worked.
build-reports/*
!build-reports/README.md

# Windows shortcuts. A .lnk stores an absolute path from the machine that made
# it, so it is useful on exactly one computer and confusing on every other.
*.lnk

# Pre-publication backup bundle and the one-shot rewrite script.
_prepublish-backup/
.prepublish-rewrite.sh
